<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cloistered-rule">Glossary</a> · Term · government</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cloistered rule</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1086">1086</a>–<a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Insei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cloistered rule, or insei, was a form of government in Heian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in which an emperor abdicated but kept power, while his chosen successor performed the ceremonial duties of the monarchy. As a system it usually refers to the practice begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shirakawa">Emperor Shirakawa</a>, who abdicated in 1086 in favour of his four-year-old son, and followed by his successors until the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> in 1192. The retired emperors, some of whom entered Buddhist monastic life, acted to counterbalance the Fujiwara regents and the warrior class, and kept courts and troops of their own. Their military units helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-clan">Taira clan</a> rise to power, and the system lingered formally long after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokyu-war">Jōkyū War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cloistered-rule.jpg" alt="Cloistered rule" width="330" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 高階隆兼（模写：板橋貫雄） · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:白河上皇の御幸.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
